### 2.1 Formal Definition of Medical Agents

We define a *medical agent* as a computational system 𝒜=(𝒫,ℛ,ℒ,ℳ,𝒯,ℱ)\mathcal{A}=(\mathcal{P},\mathcal{R},\mathcal{L},\mathcal{M},\mathcal{T},\mathcal{F}) composed of six functional modules: perception 𝒫\mathcal{P}, reasoning ℛ\mathcal{R}, planning ℒ\mathcal{L}, memory ℳ\mathcal{M}, tool use 𝒯\mathcal{T}, and action reflection ℱ\mathcal{F} [[246](https://arxiv.org/html/2607.11175v1#bib.bib246), [265](https://arxiv.org/html/2607.11175v1#bib.bib265), [206](https://arxiv.org/html/2607.11175v1#bib.bib206)]. A defining feature of this setting is *partial observability*: the agent never observes the patient’s underlying condition directly, but only indirect and incomplete evidence such as images, prior reports, and tool outputs, from which it must maintain and progressively refine an internal estimate of the true clinical state. The six modules jointly realize this process: perception and memory build and retain a belief over the latent condition, reasoning and planning turn that belief into actions such as tool calls and report operations, tool use executes them against the clinical environment, and action reflection revises the belief from returned feedback. This formulation makes explicit that medical agency is fundamentally a problem of acting under uncertainty and incomplete evidence rather than single pass prediction.

